the oracle's brief and its reply are on the row, and a count counts its own predicate

R11. The operator, 2026-09-21: *"all i wanted to see is what was sent to oracle and
what it replied (still can't btw)."* They could not, and the reason is worse than a
missing column: the column built to hold the brief existed and held **something
else**, and the reply had nowhere to go at all. The exchange lived for the duration
of one HTTP call and was then gone, so a corpus row's label could not be traced back
to the bytes it was read out of — and the rows this matters for are exactly the ones
the operator and the model disagree on, 1,294 of them, their own words: *"the rows a
fine-tune is for."* R15, the operator's ruling on an edit card's label, is folded in
at the end because it is four lines in the same file's vicinity and one commit is
cheaper than two.

## Four provenance claims, checked against the store and not the wire type

§R11 was first written from `ModelAdvice` alone and got all four wrong. Each is
re-verified here by query against `~/.local/share/letibot/sessions.db`, because the
fix depends on the correction — the corrected reading is *smaller* than the original,
and it moves the work from "add a store" to "make one column hold what its own doc
says".

1. **`brief_sha` is not a hash.** One distinct value in 6,348 rows —
   `brief/2026-09-14` — because `corpus.rs:172` writes `row.brief_format`, a *format
   version* (`BRIEF_FORMAT`). Nothing was ever hashed, so it cannot tell two briefs
   apart; and the earlier claim that the brief was "hashed and thrown away" is wrong
   in the other direction too, since it was never identified at all.
2. **`oracle_ms` is not oracle latency.** `corpus.rs:161` writes
   `Some(row.decision_ms)` — the whole-gate stopwatch. Measured: `NULL` on **0**
   rows, `0` on **5,681 of 6,348**, max **300,000 ms** against a 20,000 ms budget,
   which no oracle call could be. Any arithmetic that reads it as "the oracle ran" is
   measuring the gate; the 486-vs-633 reading this document used to carry is
   discarded with it.
3. **The column built to hold the brief held the operator's card.** `shown` is
   documented as *"the bytes the oracle was given, verbatim."* Measured before this
   commit: set on **147** rows, of which **147 were rows a person answered**, and
   `NULL` on all 486 rows an oracle decided. The 147 are recognisable as cards because
   their first line is `decision <req-id> — …`, which is `req.brief()`
   (`adjudicate.rs:910`). Cause, one line in the gate: it asked its **decider** for
   `last_brief`, and at a supervised point the decider is the person.
4. **`consulted` was not a column at all**, though its own doc calls it *"load-bearing
   for the corpus"* — so the only way to ask whether a model spoke was a regex over
   `model_verdict` prose, which `store.rs` warns against in as many words.

## And a fifth thing, which is a bug and not a provenance error

**`oracle.rs` filed a correct `UNSURE` as a parse failure.** `parse` reads the **last**
non-empty line; the guard one branch down tested `raw.trim()` — the **whole**
multi-line reply — against `"UNSURE"`, failed on exactly the shape the prompt asks
for, and reported *"gave no verdict this seam could read"* with the model's own
reasoning quoted back as noise.

    The operator asked for tests; this runs them.
    UNSURE

Measured: **39 of the 69 unreadable replies in the corpus are this shape**, so 39 rows
called the guard's answer unparseable when it had answered. The three `Unsure`s are
now told apart rather than collapsed — the model **answered** `UNSURE`, the two scores
landed **between the thresholds**, and the bytes were **no verdict at all** — and
`verdict_line` (the last non-empty line) is one function, because the defect was two
places disagreeing about which line a verdict is on.

A second, smaller defect of the same family: `Budgeted` did not forward `last_advice`,
so a budgeted gate reported that no model spoke about a call it had just consulted a
model about. A wrapper that answers for the adjudicator inside it has to answer for
*all* of it.

## What the row carries now

- **`shown` holds the brief**, which is what its doc always said. The gate now asks
  the adjudicator that **actually consulted an oracle** (`file_exchange`): the advisor
  when the advisor was put to this call, else the decider when the decider is itself a
  model, and **nothing** otherwise — a card is not the bytes an oracle was given.
- **`oracle_reply` is new (schema v9)** and holds the reply **verbatim**, captured
  before anything reads it. A reply this seam could not parse is the one most worth
  reading afterwards, so it is kept whether or not anything was made of it.
- **The model's own verdict admits and that path filed nothing at all.** It returned
  before the gate ever reached its decider, so the brief that rendered and the reply
  that admitted it were dropped, and `shown` kept whatever the *previous* call had put
  there. Measured: **486 rows whose `verdict_by` names an oracle, and not one of them
  had a brief** (520 such rows by the end of the day, 34 of them written by a build
  with this fix). That path files now.
- **`consulted INTEGER` is new (schema v10)**, written from `ModelAdvice::consulted`
  and `NULL`-able on purpose: a row written before this cannot say, and *"we did not
  keep it"* must not read as *"no model was asked"*. `SCHEMA_VERSION` 8 → 10 in the
  same commit; each step is additive and guarded by a `pragma_table_info` check,
  because migration arms run in order and the store that took v9 must still take v10.
- **The wire gains `ModelAdvice::consulted`**, `#[serde(default)]`, **no
  `PROTOCOL_VERSION` bump** — an added, defaulted field on an existing variant, the
  argument this file already makes twice. Without it `would: "unavailable"` with
  nothing behind it rendered as `model says unavailable: …`, which claims a model
  spoke; the two heads that draw it now say `no model verdict — …` instead.

## The predicates, which were measuring something else than their names

`decision_source.rs` kept its own copies of two `WHERE` clauses and both had drifted:

- `measured: oracle_ms IS NOT NULL` matched **every row** — the column is `0`, never
  NULL — so the scoped banner announced that every decision in a session had been
  measured against a model.
- `disagreements: operator_kind IS NOT NULL` counted `upheld`, which is the operator
  **agreeing**. Measured: `1863` reported where the true figure is **`1,294`**
  (`granted` 1,289 + `revoked` 5).

Both are now interpolated from one definition in the store crate — `ASKED_SQL`,
`MEASURED_SQL`, `DISAGREEMENT_SQL`, `MODEL_DECIDED_SQL` — because **a count is not
measuring what its NAME says, it is measuring what its PREDICATE says.** A linked
consequence: `measured` was `verdict_by LIKE 'model%'`, which is exact for *a model
decided* and still not *an oracle was consulted* — at `/supervised` the guard speaks
and a person decides, so that predicate silently dropped every row where the oracle
was overruled. So there are five counts now, and the fifth is the old predicate under
its own name (`CorpusCounts::model_decided`), which is also the one count that is
exact on rows written before v10. Both `/gate corpus` and the config disclosure name
all five.

## Two columns with no producer, said out loud

`p_allow` is NULL on every row because the oracle this build ships answers with a hard
label and the logprob encoding that would give it a number is not built;
`oracle_model` is left NULL **deliberately**, because the model is already named in
full on `verdict_by` (`model oracle \`qwen-3.8-27b\` at 127.0.0.1:8080 (budget
20000ms, verdict only)`) and splitting prose to recover a field is what this store's
own comment refuses. Rather than leave two columns silently empty, `/gate corpus` now
says both are producer-less and that a reader must not read their `NULL` as a
measurement.

## R15 — an edit card's label is the file, with no elided-array placeholder

    ▸ Edited […] letibot/crates/harnessd/src/answers.rs · ok · 3ms · 10 lines

The placeholder sat in the most valuable position on the row while the "more" it
pointed at was **the diff directly underneath it**. The operator's ruling: *"`[…]`
should be gone for Edit."* **Not moved — gone.**

**Where the line is drawn: on the arguments, not on the tool.** A nested value is
elided **only when the map has no scalar at all** — which keeps every case where the
elision earns its keep (a label that would otherwise be *empty*) and stops spending a
token on a row that already names something. It is on the arguments because the whole
rule is derived, and a per-tool table is what `display_target` exists to avoid. **No
seated tool in this tree has that shape today** — every one carries a scalar `path`,
`pattern` or `cmd` — so that arm is reachable by a tool added later, and a card with
no label at all still falls back to the call id rather than to a blank.

**What was deliberately not changed:** the `SUBJECT_KEYS` prepend. It answers the
other failure — the argument budget breaking *before* `path`, leaving a card that says
nothing about which file — and reordering it was proposed and rejected in favour of
removal: on an edit nothing else belongs first, so the row should not need a rule to
put the file there.

## Tests

- `the_corpus_keeps_the_oracles_brief_and_reply_not_the_persons_card` — the two
  defects in one place: both halves come from the **advisor** (the fixture stamps its
  brief with its own id, so a card cannot be mistaken for it), a person's answer files
  **nothing** in either column, and the auto-admit path that used to file nothing now
  files both.
- `unsure_is_read_from_the_last_line_and_is_not_a_parse_failure` — the three `Unsure`s
  asserted apart, including the whitespace a model really emits, and `ALLOWANCE`
  still not reading as an `ALLOW`.
- `the_oracles_brief_and_reply_round_trip_and_an_older_row_says_null` — the round
  trip, and `NULL` for *"recorded before this was kept"* told apart from *"the oracle
  said nothing"*.
- `counts_separate_who_decided_from_what_was_measured` — the third axis, and the
  fixture row where a `model_verdict` was written by the gate with nobody asked.
- `a_nested_argument_is_elided_only_when_nothing_else_can_be_shown` (R15) replaces
  `a_nested_argument_is_elided_rather_than_dumped`, whose asserted `"a.rs […]"` was
  the defect rather than the expectation.

`cargo test`: letibot-tokencore 59, letibot-tools 610, letibot-harnessd 113,
letibot-sessionlog 95, letibot-tui 297 (4 ignored). `cargo check --workspace
--all-targets` clean. Every touched file checked against rustfmt by diffing a
temp-format rather than trusting a bulk `--check`, which reports pre-existing drift.

## Not in this commit

**R12** — an oracle that ran out of budget is not one that could not be read. 11 of
the 69 unreadable replies were truncated by `max_tokens 120`, which is a budget
question and not a parsing one, and this is the next entry in the queue. The brief and
the reply are now recorded, which is what makes that one measurable.

**leticl's side of both.** R15 and R11 are requirements in both trees; this commit is
the `letibot` head's. `oracle_model` is left producer-less on purpose (above), and
making it live would need an id accessor on `AuthorisationOracle`.
